# Code Documentation

Document a method or function with a JSDoc block **only when its complexity justifies it**. Do not add comments that restate the obvious.

## When documentation is required

Add a JSDoc comment if any of the following apply:

- Non-obvious business rules, invariants, or side effects
- Non-trivial algorithms, branching, or multi-step orchestration
- Non-obvious parameters, return values, or error cases
- Private helpers whose purpose is not clear from the name alone

## When documentation is not required

Skip JSDoc for:

- Simple getters, setters, and one-liners
- CRUD/repository methods whose name already describes the action (`findById`, `save`)
- Controllers that only delegate to a use case
- Obvious `execute` methods that only call one domain factory + one repository method

## Format

Use JSDoc (`/** ... */`) in English. Describe **why / what is non-obvious**, not a line-by-line walkthrough. Prefer `@param` / `@returns` / `@throws` only when they add clarity.

```typescript
/**
 * Publishes the post only when title and content are present.
 * Returns a new instance; the original remains unpublished.
 *
 * @throws {Error} when the post is incomplete
 */
publish(): Post {
  if (!this.title || !this.content) {
    throw new Error('No se puede publicar un post incompleto');
  }
  return new Post(
    this.id,
    this.title,
    this.content,
    this.slug,
    true,
    this.createdAt,
    new Date(),
  );
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Unnecessary — name already explains the behavior
/** Finds a post by its id */
async findById(id: string): Promise<Post | null> {
  // ...
}
```
